Cloud computing is a technology that enables users to access and store data, and run applications over the internet, rather than on local computers or servers. Instead of having physical hardware or software on-site, cloud computing allows users to use remote servers hosted on the internet to manage their data, applications, and computing tasks. It offers scalability, flexibility, and cost-effectiveness, as users can scale their resources up or down based on their needs and only pay for what they use. Examples of cloud computing services include cloud storage, cloud-based applications (like Google Docs or Microsoft Office 365), and cloud hosting platforms.



Cloud computing means using the internet to store data, run programs, or do other stuff instead of doing it all on your own computer. It's like renting space and services on the internet rather than buying and maintaining your own hardware and software.